<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second United Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1937">1937</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second United Front was the alliance between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Chinese Nationalist Party</a> (Kuomintang) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> against the Japanese invasion, formed in 1937 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian-incident">Xi'an Incident</a> of December 1936. The Communists placed their forces under nominal Nationalist command as the Eighth Route Army and New Fourth Army, and their base became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaan-gan-ning-border-region">Shaan-Gan-Ning Border Region</a>. Cooperation broke down after the New Fourth Army Incident of 1941, and civil war resumed after 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-united-front.jpg" alt="Second United Front" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 中華民國國防部 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China_Army.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From late 1935 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> secretly sought Soviet aid against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> made a ceasefire with the CCP a condition. Meanwhile the CCP signed secret ceasefires with the Nationalist generals besieging them in the north-west, Zhang Xueliang of the Northeastern Army and Yang Hucheng of the Northwestern Army, who resented Chiang's preference for civil war over resistance to Japan.</p><p>Chiang still hoped for a military victory, encouraged when Hui cavalry allied with him cut up two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> corps in the Ningxia campaign in October 1936. But at the Battle of Shanchengbao the Northeastern Army held back most of its forces, and the Red Army nearly wiped out a regiment of Hu Zongnan's army. On 12 December 1936 Zhang and Yang seized Chiang to force a truce with the CCP.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The CCP accepted Chiang's leadership and some financial support from the central government. Its leaders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, sat in the new People's Political Council, intellectuals such as Guo Moruo joined the Political Department of the Military Affairs Commission, and in August 1937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> and Shao Lizi agreed that the Xinhua Daily could be published in Nationalist areas. The two sides fought together at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, and their cooperation reached its height at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> in 1938.</p><p>In practice the front worked more as a non-aggression pact than an alliance: Communist forces acted independently, and coordination was minimal. Within the CCP, Mao wanted autonomy while his rival Wang Ming favoured doing everything through the united front. By late 1938 the alliance was breaking down as both sides competed for territory in Free China; in June 1939, according to Chiang's diary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> troops under He Long destroyed a Chinese militia brigade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the New Fourth Army Incident the two parties fought each other behind Japanese lines, the CCP destroying or absorbing Nationalist partisan forces or driving them into the collaborationist army. The CCP built influence through rural mass organisations and land and tax reforms favouring poor peasants, while many Nationalist divisions were tied down blockading their areas. Peace talks between Chiang and Mao after the war failed; by 1946 civil war was general, and in October 1949 Mao proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> as Chiang retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second United Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Communist Party's own history, Mao Zedong's party reached an agreement with the Kuomintang after years of civil war and Japanese aggression to cooperate on the basis of Sun's Principles, which became known as the <b>Second United Front</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Second United Front</b> in 1937 the remaining soviet became the Shaan-Gan-Ning Border Region.</blockquote><a href="/state/chinese-soviet-republic">Chinese Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
